---
import Doc from '../../layouts/Doc.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { cliHelp, version, docHref } from '../../lib/site';
const options = cliHelp
  .slice(
    cliHelp.indexOf('Requests:'),
    cliHelp.indexOf('\nCurlimp supports a subset'),
  )
  .trim();
---

<Doc
  title="CLI options reference"
  slug="cli-reference"
  section="Usage"
  description="The supported curlimp options for requests, transport, output and write-out. Check exact flags and limits when using Curlimp in scripts."
>
  <h2>Command shape</h2>
  <CodeBlock
    code={
      'curlimp [options] [PROFILE] <URL> [options]\ncurlimp --help\ncurlimp --version'
    }
    lang="text"
    label="Command syntax"
  />
  <p>
    One HTTP(S) URL per invocation. With <a href={docHref('docker')}>Docker</a>,
    put the same request options after the image name.
  </p>
  <h2>Supported options</h2>
  <p>
    This reference is read directly from the CLI help source during the site
    build, for v{version}.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={options} lang="text" label="CLI help" />
  <h2>Response export extensions</h2>
  <p>
    <code>--txt</code>, <code>--json-output</code>, and <code>--json-file</code>
    export the selected response. They are project extensions;{' '}
    <code>--json DATA</code> still sends a request body. See the{' '}
    <a href={docHref('response-exports')}>export guide</a> for field selection,
    Docker mounts, encoding, limits, and option conflicts.
  </p>
  <h2>Write-out variables</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Variable</th>
        <th>Meaning</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>http_code</code>, <code>response_code</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          Last received HTTP response status, or 000 when none was received.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>url_effective</code>
        </td>
        <td>Final effective URL after any followed redirects.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>content_type</code>
        </td>
        <td>Received response content type, if available.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>num_redirects</code>
        </td>
        <td>Number of redirects followed.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>http_version</code>
        </td>
        <td>Reported negotiated HTTP version.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>time_total</code>
        </td>
        <td>Total transfer time in seconds.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>exitcode</code>
        </td>
        <td>Transfer result, including fail-with-body handling.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    <code>%%</code> prints a percent sign. The format supports <code>\n</code>,{' '}
    <code>\r</code> and <code>\t</code>. <code>-w @file</code> reads a format
    from a file. Variables not listed here fail explicitly; do not assume the
    entire curl write-out catalog exists.
  </p>
  <h2>Output and errors</h2>
  <p>
    Without an export mode, response bodies stream to stdout or <code>-o</code>,
    without conversion to text. Native diagnostics and CLI errors go to stderr.{' '}
    <code>-s</code> hides error messages; <code>-S</code> shows them again when
    used with <code>-s</code>. This CLI has no progress meter.
  </p>
  <p>
    Typical codes include <code>2</code> for invalid options, <code>22</code>{' '}
    for HTTP failure under fail flags, <code>23</code> for an output write
    error, and <code>26</code> for a request-input read error. Native transport
    errors retain their libcurl error codes.
  </p>
  <h2>Unsupported is explicit</h2>
  <p>
    Multiple URLs, <code>--next</code>, <code>--config</code>,{' '}
    <code>.curlrc</code>, URL globbing, multipart uploads, retries and parallel
    transfers are not implemented. <code>-q</code> is accepted, but there is no
    config-file loading to disable.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read the{' '}
    <a href={docHref('compatibility')}>complete scope and validation notes</a>{' '}
    before using the CLI as a curl replacement.
  </p>
</Doc>
